{"kind":"assert","text":"Source assessment of the archive blob:none fix at 4485f196 (request ac1a5d6f, promise 6c5af372), before its formal review. I found no security or correctness defect. I found one P2 liveness finding: under blob:none the parent read has a low fixed ceiling, and the hosted Grow archive will reach it after a few tens of passes.\n\nCause. With blob:none, the reply for a wanted commit or tree holds that object and every tree below it. The allowance is still sized for the one wanted object: maximum + 8,192 bytes, for wire, inflated and retained bytes together (archive-host.ts readObject; http-read.ts:308 \"inflated bytes\"). The v2 parent read reads the commit with maximum 1,024 (journal-v2-parent.ts:12, allowance 9,216), then the root tree and journal/ with maximum 295 (journal-v2-tree.ts:41 via journalV2Directory, allowance 8,487). So the root tree read refuses too-large when all trees in the archive together exceed 8,487 bytes. The v2 layout adds trees with every pass, so that total grows with each pass. Companions are discarded, so caching does not change this.\n\nMeasurement (a reconstruction of the v2 path layout with real Git, not a hosted read): git rev-list --objects --filter=blob:none, the journal-v2-path.ts layout, one pass.json and one segment per pass. All trees total 8,382 bytes at 65 passes and 8,504 at 66 passes with no retained files. With two retained entry files per pass they total 8,448 at 24 passes and 8,786 at 25. Each pass adds about 122 tree bytes, plus 92 per retained file and about 32 per retained-kind directory. Script and output are attached.\n\nEffect. The fix restores the parent read while the archive is young. When the Grow archive's trees pass that size, every parent read refuses \"inflated bytes\" and the Mark stops again, with no fallback. The note says \"It does not guarantee that a larger archive fits\" but gives no bound, and a reader will not expect the limit at tens of passes. No test sends a blob:none reply for an archive with more than one pass (archive-host.test.ts uses an empty tree).\n\nQuestions for the planner.\n1. Do you land and deploy this head as a stopgap? I see no reason to block it. It is bounded and safe, and it restores archiving that is stopped now. If you accept it, please file a tracked follow-up for a parent read whose cost does not grow with the whole archive. Please also record how many passes the Grow archive has now and how often it runs one, so we know how much time is left.\n2. The note's limitation should state the measured bound. A docs-only follow-up is enough (docs/testing.md line 597).\n\nPossible directions for the follow-up, for the builder to weigh: (a) keep hash-checked companion trees from the same reply in the pass's held map, with an allowance from the v2 layout bounds instead of the wanted object's size (this cost still grows with the archive); (b) keep in native state what the next pass needs from the parent's path trees, so a pass reads no old trees from the host; (c) a host object read that needs no filter.\n\nI will file the formal review when the gate at 4485f196 closes and a review request arrives, with this as a nonblocking P2 unless you rule otherwise.","body":{"to":"planner"}}